"sections": [
{
"title": "Before You Start",
"steps": [
{
"text": "Drain the beef. Pour off any liquid in the package and pat the meat dry with paper towels."
},
{
"text": "Prep the vegetables. Dice the onion into 1/4-inch cubes and mince the garlic."
},
{
"text": "Grate the mozzarella. Grate the full 12 ounces from a block; set 4 ounces aside for the top."
}
]
},
{
"title": "Make the Meat Sauce",
"steps": [
{
"text": "Cook the onion. Heat the olive oil in a large heavy-bottomed pot over medium heat. Add the onion and cook 2–3 minutes, until it starts to soften.",
"timer": 2
},
{
"text": "Brown the beef. Add the garlic and ground beef. Break the meat apart as it cooks, about 8–10 minutes, until no pink remains (160°F).",
"timer": 8
},
{
"text": "Skim the fat. Tilt the pot and spoon off any extra fat into a heatproof container."
},
{
"text": "Add the tomatoes. Stir in the diced tomatoes with their juice, the tomato sauce and the water."
},
{
"text": "Season. Add the bay leaves, basil, sugar, Italian seasoning and salt, and stir well."
},
{
"text": "Simmer. Bring to a boil, then turn the heat to low. Simmer uncovered 1–2 hours, stirring every 15–20 minutes, until very thick and no longer watery.",
"timer": 60
},
{
"text": "Keep an eye on it. Add a splash of water if it starts to stick. If oil pools on top, lay a metal spoon on the surface and let the oil run into it."
},
{
"text": "Finish the sauce. Remove and discard the bay leaves. Taste and add salt if needed."
}
]
},
{
"title": "Make the Cheese Filling",
"steps": [
{
"text": "Mix the cheeses. While the sauce simmers, stir the ricotta, 8 ounces mozzarella, Parmesan, basil, parsley, salt and pepper together in a bowl. Set aside."
}
]
},
{
"title": "Cook the Noodles",
"steps": [
{
"text": "Preheat the oven. Set it to 375°F with a rack in the middle."
},
{
"text": "Boil the noodles. Bring a large pot of generously salted water to a rolling boil. Cook the noodles about 2/3 of the package time (around 7 minutes); they should bend but still be firm.",
"timer": 7
},
{
"text": "Cool the noodles. Drain and place them in a large bowl of cold water so they don't stick together."
}
]
},
{
"title": "Layer the Lasagna",
"steps": [
{
"text": "Start with sauce. Spread 1/2 cup sauce over the bottom of a 9 × 13-inch dish so the noodles don't stick."
},
{
"text": "First layer. Lay 5–6 noodles to cover the bottom, then spread half the cheese filling and one third of the sauce."
},
{
"text": "Second layer. Add noodles, the rest of the cheese filling and another third of the sauce."
},
{
"text": "Top layer. Add the last noodles, then the remaining sauce, spreading it to the edges so the noodles don't dry out."
},
{
"text": "Add the cheese. Sprinkle the 4 ounces mozzarella evenly over the top."
}
]
},
{
"title": "Bake",
"steps": [
{
"text": "Bake covered. Cover with foil sprayed with cooking spray so it won't stick to the cheese. Bake at 375°F for 45 minutes.",
"timer": 45
},
{
"text": "Brown the top. Remove the foil and bake 15 minutes more, until the cheese is browned and the edges are bubbling.",
"timer": 15
},
{
"text": "Rest. Cover loosely with foil and let it rest 20–30 minutes so it slices cleanly.",
"timer": 20
},
{
"text": "Serve. Sprinkle with dried basil or parsley, cut into squares and serve."
}
]
}
],
"notes": [
{
"title": "Options",
"sub": "",
"items": [
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Cottage cheese: Swap 2 cups cottage cheese for the ricotta, 1 to 1. Everything else stays the same."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Italian sausage: Swap beef or turkey Italian sausage (sweet or spicy) for the ground beef. Check the label and casing for any pork."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Ground bison: Swap the same amount of ground bison for the beef."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Want it spicier: Add 1/2–1 teaspoon red pepper flakes to the sauce, or use spicy beef or turkey sausage. Use less or skip if you're sensitive to heat."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Soak instead of boil: Cover the noodles with hot (not boiling) tap water for about 30 minutes until pliable, then layer as usual. A reader tip that helps avoid soupy lasagna."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Noodles: Use regular noodles. Oven-ready noodles are thinner, soak up too much sauce and can make it dry and starchy."
}
],
"family": false
},
{
"title": "Tips for Success",
"sub": "",
"items": [
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Simmer the sauce long: 1–2 hours until very thick concentrates the flavor and keeps the lasagna from being soupy."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Make the sauce ahead: It tastes even better made 12–24 hours early. Reheat it before layering so the lasagna bakes faster."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Undercook the noodles: Most cook 9–11 minutes; pull them at about 7. They finish in the oven and stay firm instead of mushy."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Skip fresh mozzarella: It adds too much water. Grate a block of low-moisture mozzarella; pre-shredded doesn't melt as well. If you do use fresh, slice it thin and press it dry between paper towels."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Why 375°F: Hot enough to brown the top while the noodles cook evenly."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Let it rest: 20–30 minutes lets it settle so you get clean slices. It stays plenty hot."
}
],
"family": false
},
{
"title": "Make Ahead, Store and Reheat",
"sub": "",
"items": [
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Make ahead: The sauce tastes even better made a day ahead. You can also assemble the whole lasagna up to 24 hours early, cover and refrigerate; add a few extra minutes to the bake."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Leftovers: Store airtight in the dish or as single slices in the fridge, 3–5 days."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Reheat: Microwave single slices. For more than a few slices, cover with foil and bake at 350°F until hot all the way through."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Freeze: Wrap the unbaked or baked lasagna well in plastic wrap AND foil; freeze up to 2 months. A disposable 9 × 13 foil pan works well."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Bake from frozen (unbaked): Unwrap, cover with fresh foil, bake at 300°F for 1 hour, then at 375°F for 30 minutes, uncovering at the end to brown the cheese."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Reheat from frozen (baked): Unwrap, cover with fresh foil and bake at 350°F until warm all the way through."
}
],
"family": false
},
{
"title": "Clean-Eating and Serving Notes",
"sub": "",
"items": [
{
"t": "li",
"text": "No pork: Skip regular pork Italian sausage. Use beef or turkey sausage with a clean casing."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Cheese labels: Choose cheese made with microbial or vegetable rennet when you can. Kosher certification helps but isn't required."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Serve with: Garlic bread, Hasselback cheesy garlic bread, cheesy garlic pull-apart rolls, Caprese salad or Caesar salad."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Caesar dressing: Anchovies have fins and scales, so they're clean; just check the label for any other hidden ingredients."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Feast suggestions: A great make-ahead dish for Shabbat, Shavuot or Sukkot. Not for the Passover and Unleavened Bread menu."
}
],
"family": false
}
],
