"sections": [
{
"title": "Beef Bacon Jam",
"steps": [
{
"text": "Cook the chopped beef bacon in a skillet over medium heat until crisp, about 8 minutes. Move it to a plate. Leave about 2 tablespoons of fat in the pan.",
"timer": 8
},
{
"text": "Add the diced onion and cook over medium-low, stirring often, until deep golden, 15–20 minutes. Add the garlic for the last minute.",
"timer": 15
},
{
"text": "Stir in the brown sugar, coffee, vinegar, maple syrup, pepper, and the cooked bacon."
},
{
"text": "Simmer on low for 10–15 minutes, until thick and jammy. Pulse a few times in a food processor for a spreadable jam, or leave it chunky.",
"timer": 10
}
]
},
{
"title": "French Onion Spread",
"steps": [
{
"text": "Melt the butter in a small pan over medium-low. Add the onion and salt and cook slowly until soft and caramelized, about 15–20 minutes. Let it cool.",
"timer": 15
},
{
"text": "Stir the cooled onions into the cream cheese, sour cream, Worcestershire, and garlic powder. Chill until serving."
}
]
},
{
"title": "Burgers",
"steps": [
{
"text": "<b>Drain the meat.</b> Put the ground bison in a colander over the sink for a few minutes to let the juices run off, then pat it dry with paper towels."
},
{
"text": "<b>Shape.</b> Gently form 4 thick patties, about 1 inch thick and slightly wider than the buns, without overworking the meat. Press a shallow dimple in the center of each. Season both sides with the salt, pepper, and garlic powder."
},
{
"text": "<b>Cook.</b> Heat the oil or butter in a cast-iron skillet over medium-high. Cook the patties 5–6 minutes per side (about 10–12 minutes total for a 1-inch patty), until the center reads 160°F. Not there yet? Turn the heat to medium, cover, and check every 2 minutes. Never serve ground bison below 160°F.",
"timer": 5
},
{
"text": "<b>Cheese.</b> In the last minute, add a slice of fontina to each patty and cover the pan to melt it."
},
{
"text": "<b>Onion.</b> Lay the sliced onion rings in the same pan for 1–2 minutes, just enough to soften them slightly, or leave them raw for a sharper bite.",
"timer": 1
},
{
"text": "<b>Toast the buns.</b> Butter the cut sides and toast them in the pan until golden, about 1 minute.",
"timer": 1
},
{
"text": "<b>Build.</b> Bottom bun, a thick layer of French onion spread, the burger with melted fontina, a big spoonful of beef bacon jam, onion, and the top bun."
}
]
}
],
"notes": [
{
"title": "Clean-Eating Notes",
"sub": "",
"items": [
{
"t": "li",
"text": "<b>Bacon jam:</b> Restaurant bacon jam is made with pork. This recipe uses beef bacon only. Never use pork bacon, even if the package says kosher."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "<b>For my son:</b> Turkey bacon works in the jam too. It's leaner and cooks faster, so add a little butter or oil to the pan in step 1."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "<b>Worcestershire sauce:</b> Most brands, like Lea & Perrins, contain anchovies. Anchovies have fins and scales, so they are clean. Read the label to make sure no other seafood is listed."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "<b>Buns:</b> A few bakery brioche buns use lard. Check the label, or pick a kosher-certified brand if one is easy to find."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "<b>Cheese on meat:</b> Allowed in this book."
}
],
"family": false
},
{
"title": "Make It Easier",
"sub": "",
"items": [
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Make the bacon jam and onion spread a day ahead. On burger night you only cook the patties."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Short on time? A good store-bought French onion dip can stand in for the homemade spread. Check the label first."
}
],
"family": false
},
{
"title": "Variations",
"sub": "",
"items": [
{
"t": "li",
"text": "<b>The older Esquire version:</b> An earlier version of their menu served the bison burger with white cheddar, pickled red onion, lettuce, and remoulade on a sesame bun (<a href=\"https://places.singleplatform.com/esquire-tavern-the/menu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SinglePlatform menu listing</a>). If that's the one you had, swap those toppings in."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "<b>Buffalo-sauce burger:</b> Brush the cooked patties with hot sauce mixed with a little melted butter. Top with pepper jack and ranch or blue cheese dressing."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "<b>Kid-friendly sliders:</b> Make small patties with American cheese and turkey bacon, and skip the onion."
}
],
"family": false
},
{
"title": "On the Side",
"sub": "",
"items": [
{
"t": "p",
"text": "The Esquire Tavern serves its burgers with chili-salt fries and cumin aioli (<a href=\"https://esquiretavern-sa.com/wp-content/uploads/Esquire-Food-Menu.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Esquire Tavern menu</a>). For an easy home version, toss hot fries with salt, chili powder, and a pinch of cumin. For the dip, stir a little cumin, lime, and garlic into mayo."
}
],
"family": false
},
{
"title": "Feast Suggestions",
"sub": "",
"items": [
{
"t": "li",
"text": "<b>Sukkot:</b> A great meal to cook outdoors and eat in the sukkah with family."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "<b>Shavuot:</b> Fits right in with the creamy, cheesy dishes of this feast."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "<b>Shabbat:</b> Make the jam and spread ahead and serve as a Friday dinner before sundown."
}
],
"family": false
}
],
