"sections": [
{
"title": "Before You Start",
"steps": [
{
"text": "Drain the chicken. Put the chicken in a colander in the sink for 3–5 minutes so the juices run off. Pat it dry on all sides with paper towels.",
"timer": 3
},
{
"text": "Cut into bite-size pieces. Cut the chicken into 1-inch cubes, about the size of the bow ties, so every bite gets chicken and pasta. Try to keep them the same size so they cook evenly."
},
{
"text": "Season. Mix the salt, pepper, Italian seasoning, paprika, and garlic powder in a small bowl. Toss it with the chicken pieces until they're evenly coated."
},
{
"text": "Start the pasta water. Fill a large pot about ¾ full of water, add 1 tablespoon salt, cover, and turn the heat to high."
},
{
"text": "Optional: Cook the Bacon",
"para": true
},
{
"text": "Crisp it. If using bacon, put it in the cold skillet and turn the heat to medium. Cook 6–8 minutes for beef bacon (4–6 for turkey bacon), stirring, until crisp. Move it to a paper towel–lined plate. Leave 1 tablespoon of grease in the pan and use it in place of 1 tablespoon of the olive oil.",
"timer": 6
}
]
},
{
"title": "Cook the Chicken",
"steps": [
{
"text": "Heat the pan. Put the skillet over medium-high heat. Add the olive oil. It's ready when it shimmers."
},
{
"text": "Sear. Add the chicken pieces in one layer, away from you so the oil doesn't splash you. Don't move them for 3 minutes, until the bottoms are golden brown.",
"timer": 3
},
{
"text": "Stir and cook. Stir and cook 4–5 more minutes, turning the pieces, until golden on most sides."
},
{
"text": "Check that it's done. Push the thermometer into the biggest piece. It's done at 165°F. No thermometer? Cut a big piece in half: no pink inside and the juices run clear. Not there yet? Cook 2 more minutes and check again."
},
{
"text": "Set it aside. Move the chicken to a plate. Don't wash the pan; the brown bits are flavor."
}
]
},
{
"title": "Cook the Pasta",
"steps": [
{
"text": "Boil the pasta. When the water is at a full rolling boil, add the bow ties and stir. Cook for the time on the box for \"al dente\" (usually 11–13 minutes), stirring every few minutes. Bow ties are done when the thick knot in the middle is tender, not chalky. Bite one to check.",
"timer": 11
},
{
"text": "Save some pasta water. Before you drain it, scoop out 1 cup of the cooking water with a measuring cup and set it aside."
},
{
"text": "Drain. Carefully pour the pasta into a colander in the sink. Steam will rush up, so pour away from your face."
}
]
},
{
"title": "Make the Sauce",
"steps": [
{
"text": "Melt the butter. Turn the skillet down to medium. Add the butter and let it melt."
},
{
"text": "Cook the tomatoes. Add the cherry tomatoes and sun-dried tomatoes. Cook 3–4 minutes, stirring, until the cherry tomatoes soften and start to wrinkle.",
"timer": 3
},
{
"text": "Add the garlic. Stir in the garlic and red pepper flakes. Cook 30–60 seconds, just until it smells good. Don't let it brown."
},
{
"text": "Add the broth. Pour in the broth and scrape the bottom of the pan with a wooden spoon to loosen the brown bits."
},
{
"text": "Add the cream. Pour in the heavy cream. Bring it to a gentle bubble (small bubbles around the edges), then turn the heat to medium-low. Simmer 3–4 minutes, stirring often, until it coats the back of a spoon.",
"timer": 3
},
{
"text": "Melt in the Parmesan. Sprinkle in the Parmesan a handful at a time, stirring after each handful until it melts and the sauce is smooth."
},
{
"text": "Wilt the spinach. Add the spinach a handful at a time and stir. It shrinks a lot. Cook about 1–2 minutes, until it's dark green and soft.",
"timer": 1
}
]
},
{
"title": "Finish",
"steps": [
{
"text": "Add the pasta and chicken. Add the drained bow ties and the chicken (and bacon, if using). Toss with tongs until everything is coated."
},
{
"text": "Fix the thickness. Too thick? Add the saved pasta water, ¼ cup at a time, until it's silky. Too thin? Let it simmer 1–2 minutes more; it thickens as it sits.",
"timer": 1
},
{
"text": "Taste. Taste it (carefully, it's hot) and add a pinch more salt or pepper if needed."
},
{
"text": "Serve. Serve right away with extra Parmesan on top."
}
]
}
],
"notes": [
{
"title": "Tips and Leftovers",
"sub": "",
"items": [
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Make it faster: Use a rotisserie chicken. Skip steps 1–10 and stir in 3 cups of shredded chicken at step 21."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "For my son: He loves it spicy, so keep the red pepper flakes in, and use turkey bacon (his favorite)."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Want it spicier? Use up to 1 teaspoon red pepper flakes, or add a pinch of cayenne with the garlic."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Sensitive to heat? Use just a pinch of red pepper flakes or leave them out."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Leftovers: Keep 3–4 days in the fridge. Reheat on the stove over low heat with a splash of milk or broth, stirring, because cream sauces thicken in the fridge."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Freezing: Cream sauces can separate when frozen, so this one is best fresh."
}
],
"family": false
},
{
"title": "Clean-Eating Notes",
"sub": "",
"items": [
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Chicken: Clean. It isn't on the forbidden bird list in VaYikra 11:13–19."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Bacon: Only beef bacon or turkey bacon. Never pork bacon, even if the package says kosher."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Chicken broth: Check the label for pork or mixed \"meat stock.\""
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Parmesan: Real Parmesan is made with animal rennet (an enzyme). Choose a kosher-certified Parmesan if you'd like to skip the question."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Cheese and cream with chicken: Allowed in this book."
}
],
"family": false
},
{
"title": "Options",
"sub": "",
"items": [
{
"t": "p",
"text": "Everything is cooked the same way; just swap one thing."
},
{
"t": "p",
"text": "Other pastas that go great with this sauce Cook any of these by the \"al dente\" time on its box, then add it at step 21 exactly the same way. Use the same amount (12 oz)."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Penne: The classic. The tubes fill up with sauce."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Rigatoni: Bigger tubes, extra hearty."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Cavatappi (corkscrews): The spirals grab the most sauce."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Rotini or fusilli: Twisty and kid-friendly, great for my son."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Campanelle: Little ruffled cones that hold the tomatoes and spinach."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Orecchiette: Small cups that scoop up the sauce."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Fettuccine or linguine: Long noodles for a restaurant feel. Toss with tongs at step 21, and add a little extra pasta water so it coats every strand."
}
],
"family": false
},
{
"title": "Chicken shape",
"items": [],
"subs": [
{
"title": "Chicken shape",
"items": [
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Sliced strips instead of cubes: Slice each breast in half the long way into 2 thin cutlets. Sear 4–5 minutes on the first side and 3–5 on the second, until 165°F. Rest 5 minutes, then slice into strips and add at step 21. This is how it looks in the original picture."
}
]
}
],
"family": false
},
{
"title": "Feast Suggestions",
"sub": "",
"items": [
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Shavuot: A rich, creamy dairy dish, perfect for this feast."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "A love-feast dish: Named for the banqueting-house of Shir HaShirim, a sweet one to make for my soon to be husband."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Shabbat: A cozy Friday dinner. Cut up the chicken and the tomatoes ahead to save time."
},
{
"t": "li",
"text": "Not for Passover / Unleavened Bread: Pasta is made from wheat."
}
],
"family": false
}
],
